#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Put MAME's frame N next to the native shell's frame N and measure the gap.

Question this answers: *where* do the two renderers disagree, not merely that
their hashes differ. It writes `side-by-side.png` (oracle left, native right,
each labelled), `diff.png` (amplified absolute per-channel difference) and
`block-map.png` (coarse difference density), and reports differing-pixel count,
differing-pixel fraction, and an 8x8 block map of the difference density.

The metric that matters most is not the differing count. It is the split of the
pixels that AGREE into `agreeing_both_black_pixels` and
`agreeing_lit_pixels`, because "the two frames agree on 3.81% of pixels" reads
very differently when all of that agreement is background black and **zero**
pixels agree on drawn content. A tool reporting one number for both hides
exactly the case worth seeing. When `agreeing_lit_pixels` is 0 and something
differs, the tool says so loudly on stdout.

Oracle acquisition, in order of preference:

  `--oracle-source fixture` (default)
      Reads the committed `fixtures/oracle/frame_600/frame_600_screen.png`.
      ROM-free, MAME-free, and committed, so this path is reproducible from a
      clean checkout. That fixture is a `service`-input frame 600; see
      `fixtures/oracle/frame_600/metadata.json`.

  `--oracle-source mame`
      Runs the pinned MAME through `tools/mame-gsp-state-snapshot` with
      `--input none` and `--targets N`. `--input none` is the only MAME input
      mode this repository treats as deterministic (AGENTS.md trap 4), and
      `--nothrottle` keeps emulated frame numbers meaningful. This path is the
      only one that can answer "does the hash recorded in
      `reference/checkpoints/title/metadata.json` still reproduce?".

  `--oracle-png PATH`
      Any PNG you already have. Use this to compare two of our own frames.

What it reports about provenance, loudly rather than silently:

  * the sha256 of the oracle image actually used;
  * whether that equals the `screenshot.sha256` recorded in the title
    checkpoint's metadata.json (it does NOT, and the tool says so on stderr);
  * whether the oracle image is byte-identical to the committed fixture.

What this tool gets wrong, and cannot see:

  * It compares ONE frame. Two frames agreeing is not evidence the emulations
    agree; it is consistent with a frozen, blank, or all-black frame on either
    side hashing just as reproducibly as a correct one. Read `lit`/`colours`
    from the native run's own `render frame=` line before believing a match.
    `agreeing_both_black_pixels` is printed for the same reason: agreement on
    black is not agreement.
  * A per-pixel difference has no notion of which side is right. The tool says
    "these disagree", never "the native side is wrong".
  * Frame N on each side is frame N of each side's OWN counter. Neither is
    anchored to the other's emulated clock, so a timing offset shows up as a
    content difference and is indistinguishable from a wrong pixel. The native
    shell must have a display (`STUNRUN_LIVE_VIDEO=1`) or it renders
    `mode=blank-scaffold` and every pixel is black: a comparison against that
    is a comparison against nothing.
  * `--threshold` is a comparison knob, not a pass/fail gate. Raising it to
    make a mismatch disappear defeats the tool; the default is exact equality.
  * It cannot read MAME's GSP VRAM/palette/registers, so it cannot separate
    "our scanout of the oracle's VRAM is wrong" from "our VRAM is wrong". For
    that split use `tools/decode-gsp-frame` and `tools/gsp_render.py`.
  * The `--check-rom-overlap` pass only searches maximal nonzero runs of length
    >= 8. A buffer built from short ROM-derived fragments, or one whose runs
    were transformed, can pass it while still being derived from ROM bytes.

Usage:

    tools/compare-frame --out OUTDIR                     # fixture oracle + native run
    tools/compare-frame --out OUTDIR --oracle-source mame \
        --rompath roms --mame /mnt/build/mame-oracle/mame
    tools/compare-frame A.png B.png --out OUTDIR         # compare two PNGs

Every wait is bounded: `--native-timeout` (default 900 s) for the shell and
`--mame-timeout` (default 900 s) for MAME. A timeout says what it was waiting
for and exits nonzero. Runs go through a private Xvfb and are reaped.
"""

